<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahad Datu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lahad Datu is a town on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> in Malaysian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, a centre of the palm-oil industry. In February 2013 more than 200 armed followers of Jamalul Kiram III, a claimant to the Sultanate of Sulu, landed nearby to press the sultanate's historic claim to Sabah, and Malaysian security forces expelled them in fighting that killed dozens.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahad-datu.jpg" alt="Lahad Datu" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LDyou2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahad_Datu_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lahad Datu is the capital of the Lahad Datu District, on the Dent Peninsula in the Tawau Division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>. The town is surrounded by stretches of cocoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> plantations and is also an important port for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> exports. Federal Route 13, part of the Pan-Borneo Highway network on the east coast of Sabah, links it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a>, Tawau and other districts, and the town has an airport for domestic flights. Its climate is tropical rainforest, with heavy rainfall all year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A settlement is believed to have existed here in the 15th century, as excavations have unearthed Chinese ceramics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. A Jawi manuscript of 1408 in the Ida'an language tells of an Ida'an man named Abdullah in Darvel Bay who embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and on its evidence the town is believed to be the first place in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> where Islam was introduced. In the 19th century the village of Tunku, just east of the town, was a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and slave traders.</p><p>On 23 September 1985, 15 to 20 armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> from the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> landed in the town, killing at least 21 people and injuring 11. The standoff of February 2013 with the self-proclaimed Royal Security Forces of the Sultanate of Sulu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> lasted over a month and reportedly left 68 dead: 56 on the Sulu side, nine members of the Malaysian forces and six civilians. Malaysia then created the Eastern Sabah Security Command and the Eastern Sabah Security Zone.</p><p>Until the incursion Malaysia had paid an annual cession payment of roughly $1,000 to the heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Sulu, honouring an agreement of 1878 by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> was conceded to a British company. After the standoff the payment was halted, and eight of the heirs, who said they had not been involved, later took legal action based on the original commercial deal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Its population was estimated at around 27,887 in 2010. The town has several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> refineries, and the Palm Oil Industrial Cluster nearby, a state-owned company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, runs its own deep-water port, which received its first vessel on 1 March 2013. Lahad Datu Port is a container port administered by Sabah Port Sdn. Bhd.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahad Datu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2013 security forces expelled armed followers of a claimant to the Sultanate of Sulu who landed at <b>Lahad Datu</b> in Sabah, and Malaysia maintains claims and patrols in the South China Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
